 ### Development
-The approach builds on the postulate that minimizing the posterior entropy $H(\mathcal{D}|\mathcal{X})$ is equivalent to maximizing the joint entropy of projections $H(\mathcal{X})$. It distinguishes itself from photographic NeRF angle selection (which separates views by $180^\circ$) by recognizing that X-ray projections at $\theta$ and $\theta+\pi$ are not identical in cone beam geometry, leading to different optimal configurations [S1, unofficial].
+The approach builds on the postulate that minimizing the posterior entropy $H(\mathcal{D}|\mathcal{X})$ is equivalent to maximizing the joint entropy of projections $H(\mathcal{X})$ [S1, unofficial]. It distinguishes itself from photographic NeRF angle selection (which separates views by $180^\circ$) by recognizing that X-ray projections at $\theta$ and $\theta+\pi$ are not identical in cone beam geometry, leading to different optimal configurations [S1, unofficial].
 
